      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Following the 1713 Treaty of Utrecht, Placentia passed from French to British hands and later hosted a naval station in the 1760s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Placentia-Newfoundland-and-Labrador",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Sir Ambrose Shea Lift Bridge in Placentia swings over the tidal 'gut' of Placentia Bay, a unique maritime landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Placentia%2C_Newfoundland_and_Labrador",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Castle Hill National Historic Park preserves the original fortifications that once guarded Placentia during the colonial wars.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Placentia-Newfoundland-and-Labrador",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Placentia Area Theatre D'Heritage brings history alive each summer with performances that dramatize the town’s colonial past.",
